$BODY = <<<'HTML'
<h2>The allegation and the arrest</h2>
<p>FIR No. 0279 of 2025 was registered at Police Station Rishikesh, District Dehradun, on 5 June 2025. It alleged that a girl of about seven years was lured into a room, shown an obscene video and inappropriately touched, with threats extended to her and her parents. The provisions invoked were Sections 351(3) and 65(1) of the Bharatiya Nyaya Sanhita, 2023, read with Sections 3 and 4 of the POCSO Act, 2012. The applicant was arrested the next day and remained in judicial custody from 6 June 2025.</p>
<p>The difficulty, as the High Court&rsquo;s order records, lay inside the FIR itself: the body of the report attributed the acts to one &ldquo;Sajjan Ram&rdquo;, while its concluding lines sought action against the applicant, Pappu Ram. Two different names, one document.</p>

<h2>How the Court weighed the material</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>The FIR&rsquo;s internal conflict.</strong> The narrative named another man; only the closing portion pointed at the applicant.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The victim&rsquo;s statement.</strong> It named the applicant — but the cross-examination of the victim&rsquo;s mother brought out circumstances concerning Sajjan Singh and his family&rsquo;s presence at a function, which the Court found did not sufficiently establish the applicant&rsquo;s involvement at this stage.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Custody and antecedents.</strong> Over seventeen months in judicial custody, with no criminal history.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>The outcome.</strong> Bail allowed on a personal bond with two sureties of like amount, to the satisfaction of the court concerned.</div>
</div>
<p>Hon&rsquo;ble Mr. Justice Pankaj Purohit, deciding the application on 17 November 2026, held that in these circumstances further detention was unwarranted. As is standard, the findings are confined to the bail stage; identity remains a matter for trial.</p>

<h2>Identity at the bail stage: the governing idea</h2>
<p>Bail courts do not conduct mini-trials, but they are entitled — indeed required — to form a prima facie view of the material. Where the question is not the quality of an admitted participant&rsquo;s conduct but whether the arrested person is the offender at all, the calculus changes. A genuine and unresolved identity doubt means the gravest allegation in the FIR may simply not belong to the person in custody. Keeping such a person imprisoned for the length of a POCSO trial, which can run for years, would make the process itself the punishment.</p>
<p>At the same time, courts remain conscious of the child victim&rsquo;s interest. Bail orders in such cases routinely carry conditions designed to keep the accused away from the victim and the witnesses, and the grant of bail neither dilutes the charge nor forecasts the verdict.</p>

<h2>Takeaways for practitioners</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Read the FIR line by line. Discrepancies between the narrative portion and the concluding prayer are not rare, and they matter most where they concern identity.</p>
  <p>Use the record, not rhetoric: in this case the decisive material was the mother&rsquo;s cross-examination, which introduced circumstances pointing away from the applicant.</p>
  <p>Custody arithmetic counts. Seventeen months of pre-trial detention, with no antecedents, is a substantial factor in the liberty balance.</p>
  <p>For complainants&rsquo; counsel, the lesson is the mirror image: ensure the FIR and the statements under investigation are consistent on identity before the case reaches a bail court.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">This article reports a judicial order for general information and does not comment on the merits of any pending trial.</div>
